# See http://help.github.com/ignore-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# ARIA Phase-1 PoC runtime output (tools/aria-poc/poc.py is committed; outputs are not)
.aria-poc/
.aria-ci/
artifacts/
.full-review/
aria-kernel/aria-tools/
# PLAN Wave 1 PR 2.6b — the `aria/state` store's worktree. `git worktree add`
# inside the checkout leaves a directory git reports as untracked (`?? `), and
# every ARIA lane runs under `clean_worktree_policy: pre_and_post`, so without
# this the durable store would fail the lane's own cleanliness gate the moment
# it was checked out. Ignoring it is correct, not a workaround: the store's
# content is committed to its OWN branch by `publish_state`, and staging any of
# it here would put the state tree in the code branch.
.aria-state-store/
# ORPHAN-HIGH-793 — the writers attestation is a DELIBERATE host-local
# SIBLING of the store directory (state_store.py: "a HOST-LOCAL sibling of
# the store directory, never a write into the store worktree itself", so
# the store's own invariants stay blind to it). The nightly's
# workspace-clean gate is NOT blind to it: for three nights it tripped on
# this one untracked file. Ignored for the same reason the store is: it is
# designed host-local runtime material, never content for the code branch.
.aria-state-store.writers.jsonl
# ARIA runtime state trees — created at runtime by the kernel/workflows
# (cycle ledgers, artifact-restored state, fresh-scan seed findings);
# runtime writes must never dirty the discovery/burn-in worktree
# (ORPHAN-LOW-306).
#
# ORPHAN-HIGH-434 — this MUST stay `aria-tools/*`, not `aria-tools/`.
# git does not descend into an excluded DIRECTORY, so with the bare
# `aria-tools/` form every negation further down this file was inert,
# including `!aria-tools/reports/daily/**`. `git add` on a daily
# chain-tip anchor exited 1 and no anchor was committed between
# 2026-05-08 and 2026-07-26 — the governance chain-tips existed only
# inside expiring CI artifacts while the workflow reported success.
# The `/*` form excludes the children instead of the directory, so the
# ancestor re-includes below can fire. Reverting this line to
# `aria-tools/` re-breaks the audit trail silently; the invariant in
# aria-kernel/tests/test_daily_report_anchor_invariant.py is what
# catches it.
aria-tools/*
# Re-include the ANCESTOR of every tracked entry, not just the leaf. git does
# not descend into an excluded directory, so a negation deeper in this file can
# only fire if each directory above it is re-included here. The tracked
# allowlist is declared below (repo_identity.json, agent-evals/fixtures/,
# reports/daily/) and enforced by
# aria-kernel/tests/test_aria_tools_tracked_allowlist.py; before this block only
# reports/daily/ actually worked, so a new eval fixture or a repo_identity
# schema bump could not be staged at all.
!aria-tools/reports/
!aria-tools/agent-evals/
!aria-tools/repo_identity.json
aria-findings/
aria-tools/.archive/
aria-tools/.backups/
# Plan 019 Phase 0.3 — recursive_impact runtime artifacts (live evidence
# already lives in runs.jsonl + governance.jsonl; per-compute graph dumps
# under aria-tools/impact-graphs/ are throwaway snapshots).
aria-tools/impact-graphs/
.claude/agents/.dispatch-log.jsonl
.claude/worktrees/
# Same runtime artifact in nested .claude/ dirs (e.g. web/apps/aquamobil/.claude/);
# line above is root-anchored, so a `**/`-prefixed pattern is needed to catch nested copies.
**/.claude/agents/.dispatch-log.jsonl
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock

